# Fixture (BH-7970): the OLD qa prompt builder, verbatim from c73cc1b2:bin/lib/rb/pr.sh line(s) 1578.
# tests/stage-prompt.bh-7970-test.sh sources it with stubs; everything after line 3 is the provenance body. Do not edit it.
# shellcheck shell=bash
QA_PROMPT_FOR() { printf 'QA stage. A Vercel PREVIEW deployment of branch %s is live at %s. ACCESS: if env var VERCEL_AUTOMATION_BYPASS_SECRET is set, use Vercel bypass COOKIE mode — navigate FIRST to "<url>/?x-vercel-protection-bypass=$SECRET&x-vercel-set-bypass-cookie=true", after which the cookie authenticates every request. NEVER send the bypass as a global playwright extraHTTPHeaders header: it rides along on third-party requests and breaks their CORS preflight (Clerk CDN lesson, 2026-07-28). SIGN-IN: if env vars QA_CLERK_SECRET_KEY and QA_TEST_USER_ID are set, authenticate as the low-privilege QA test user before driving screens: mint a one-time token (POST https://api.clerk.com/v1/sign_in_tokens with {"user_id":$QA_TEST_USER_ID,"expires_in_seconds":300}, Authorization: Bearer $QA_CLERK_SECRET_KEY) and open "<url>/sign-in?__clerk_ticket=<token>" (reference implementation: /root/harness/bin/qa-signin-check.mjs). Screens the QA role legitimately cannot access show a permission wall — note that as observed behavior, not a defect. If sign-in env is absent, QA the public surface and note authed screens as not-covered. Read %s/plan.md, especially "## QA screens". VERIFICATION MAP: if this repo has a docs/verification-map/ directory, it is the established record of how each feature is reached and driven — read the entries for the screens you are about to exercise and take their nav paths, routes, selectors and preconditions from there instead of rediscovering them, and follow their driving conventions (ARIA/data-attr selectors, poll an observable end-state rather than sleeping, evaluate only to READ state). An entry that no longer matches the app is a finding to report in qa.md, not something to quietly work around. The THIRD-TO-LAST line of qa.md must be exactly "MAP ENTRIES: <comma-separated entry files you used, or none>" — "none" is the honest answer when the repo has no map or none of its entries covered these screens. Write and run a HEADLESS playwright script (chromium; resolve playwright via createRequire from the repo, ESM ignores NODE_PATH) that, for each listed screen: loads it at 1280px AND 375px viewports, exercises the primary flow with obviously-fake test inputs on NON-DESTRUCTIVE actions only (never mutate real client data, never trigger deletes/payments/emails — previews share the PROD database), captures console errors + failed requests, and screenshots to %s/qa-shots/. Note, do not FAIL on, a missing sort indicator, a dead non-identity row, a few px of overflow or a copy nit (BH-7123). FAIL on a core flow a user cannot finish, a broken screen, a console error, a native dialog that freezes the drive. Write findings to %s/qa.md — pre-existing issues outside this ticket are noted separately, not failed. FOLLOW-UPS ARE GROUPED: a follow-up found during a build or review goes into ONE follow-up ticket per screen or flow, with every item as a checklist, never one ticket per finding (BH-6902). A follow-up ticket is filed only for a core-flow defect a user would notice (review.followup_min_level, BH-7123); smoke-level findings stay as [NOTE] lines in the review, and a ticket that is itself a follow-up files no follow-ups. COUNT WHAT YOU ACTUALLY DID: the SECOND-TO-LAST line of qa.md must be exactly "QA COVERAGE: screens=<n> flows=<n> shots=<n>", counting only screens you really loaded, flows you really drove, and screenshots really written to %s/qa-shots/ — never what the plan listed or what you intended. If you exercised zero screens (playwright would not install, every URL failed, sign-in never worked), the verdict is QA: FAIL with screens=0; a pass with nothing behind it is the one outcome that must never happen. Last line exactly "QA: PASS" or "QA: FAIL" (FAIL only for real in-scope defects, console errors, broken screens, or zero coverage).' "$BRANCH" "$1" "$BUILD" "$BUILD" "$BUILD" "$BUILD"; }
